    "round": 2,
    "role": "maker",
    "content": "Done. The bookmark manager is built, running, and verified end-to-end.\n\n## What you've got\n\nA single-user local web app in `/work`:\n\n```\nmain.py            FastAPI app + REST API\ndb.py              SQLite storage (normalized tags, auto-cleanup of orphans)\nstatic/            Frontend — index.html, style.css, app.js\nrequirements.txt   Dependencies\nREADME.md          Setup + API docs\n```\n\n## Features (all tested working)\n- **Add / edit / delete** bookmarks\n- **Auto-fetch** page title + favicon when you add a URL (verified: `example.com` → \"Example Domain\")\n- **Tags** with a clickable count-based tag cloud for filtering; unused tags are auto-pruned\n- **Full-text search** across title, URL, and notes (debounced)\n- **Import / export** as JSON\n\n## Run it\n\n```bash\ncd /work\npython3 -m venv .venv\n.venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt\n.venv/bin/uvicorn main:app --reload\n```\n\nThen open http://127.0.0.1:8000\n\n## Notes on the design choices\n- **Tags are normalized** (separate `tags` + `bookmark_tags` tables) rather than a comma string, so counts and filtering are cheap and consistent.\n- **Title-fetch is best-effort** — it never blocks adding a bookmark; if the fetch fails (offline, timeout, blocked), it falls back to the URL as the title.\n- **Search uses SQL `LIKE`** — plenty for a personal collection. If you ever want ranked/fuzzy search over thousands of entries, SQLite's FTS5 would be the upgrade path.\n\nA couple of things I deliberately kept simple that you might want next: edit currently uses browser `prompt()` dialogs (quick but plain — an inline edit form would be nicer), and there's no auth since it's local-only. Want me to polish either of those, or add anything else like drag-to-reorder, a browser-bookmarks HTML importer, or bulk operations?",
